{"content": "/**
 * @layout two-column
 * @summary Two-column layout with left and right content regions.
 * @remarks
 * ✅ Use Two-Column when:
 * - You have two content areas of roughly equal importance
 * - Comparing or contrasting two sets of content side-by-side
 * - Building a responsive layout that stacks on mobile
 * 🚫 Don't use Two-Column when:
 * - One column dominates the page — use asymmetric layout instead
 * - Content doesn't benefit from side-by-side presentation
 * @accessibility
 * - Ensure both columns are perceivable and usable on narrow viewports
 * - Use semantic landmarks (e.g., `<section>`) to wrap column content
 * - Maintain sufficient space and contrast between columns
 * @selector div[class~="pfx-two-column"]
 * @part .container - Root two-column container.
 * @part .left - Left column content area.
 * @part .right - Right column content area.
 * @part .divider - Optional visual divider between columns.
 * @related page-layout
 */

div[class~="pfx-two-column"] {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  gap: var(--instui-space-large);
  padding: var(--instui-space-large);
}

div[class~="pfx-two-column"] .pfx-left,
div[class~="pfx-two-column"] .pfx-right {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: var(--instui-space-medium);
}

div[class~="pfx-two-column"] .pfx-divider {
  display: none;
}

div[class~="pfx-two-column"] .pfx-divider.-visible {
  display: block;
  width: 1px;
  background-color: var(--instui-color-border);
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  div[class~="pfx-two-column"] {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--instui-space-medium);
  }
}
